#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204690} :activity/id #uuid "b4fb5865-3b9a-5c6d-bd3e-b4ce289f995d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048710 :topic/id #uuid "b73e30a4-d62d-555b-a6f7-1a03dfa6cf83" :topic/math-academy-id 4837} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205238 :tutorial/content "We've already seen how adjacency matrices describe finite relations. In this lesson, we'll learn other common ways to describe relations visually. \n\nSimilar to functions, binary relations can be visualized using **mapping diagrams**. \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\ndefined on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\}.$ \n\nThis relation corresponds to the diagram shown below, where the ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram.\nEach arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\n![](images/0e/0e5e4e1a1fe57428c551263959956ad0139766d5607b4afa6d918a2f121fe629.png)\n\n \n\nThe mapping diagram representation is usually used only for finite binary relations with relatively small underlying sets." :tutorial/id #uuid "f3131b0a-f923-5d84-a190-128c95e45a0e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9074 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fe03693e-6006-5f8a-85d0-c47be07217d0" :step/math-academy-id 156613 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204691}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204691 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203698 :question/id #uuid "9306fe02-b1dd-584c-852f-27778aed9d8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14650"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419024}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"} :step/id #uuid "8b30856e-ec75-50dc-a46d-44fced53e002" :step/math-academy-id 156614 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204692}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204692 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205232 :tutorial/content "Binary relations can be visualized using a **plot** (or **graph**). \n\nFor example, consider the binary relation $R$ defined over $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ and $B = \\{ 4, 5 \\},$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,4), (1,5), (2, 4), (3,5) \\},\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ corresponds to the plot shown below, where \n\n- the elements from the first set are written along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the elements from the second set are written along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)\\in R$ defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\n![](images/ff/ff049d0c79f75a14721d02576a83b945e9cfa1485353ad30a6c77d3ac156f3e4.png)\n\n \n\nPlots usually work well for finite and infinite (discrete or continuous) relations.\n\n**Watch out!** A relation may have points that line on the same vertical line, like the points $(1,4)$ and $(1,5)$ in $R.$ In general, a relation is *not* a function. Functions are a special case of relations, where the points with the same first component but different second components are not allowed. We'll learn more about these special kinds of relations in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab28a187-5e84-5bb2-994b-680c5df7d983" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9047 :tutorial/title "Graphs of Relations"} :step/id #uuid "13d13119-2421-512a-96c3-705a3abe3474" :step/math-academy-id 156617 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203684 :question/id #uuid "53571345-258b-54e1-af9c-fa68d0d0f40e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14613"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416072}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "7ceb273c-0209-5e50-bb43-184978f7b964" :step/math-academy-id 156618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205239 :tutorial/content "Any binary relation that's defined over one set can be visualized using a **directed graph** (or **digraph**), where\n\n- the **nodes** ( or **vertices**) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an **arrow** from node $x$ to node $y$, and\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a **loop** from node $x$ to itself.\n\nFor example, consider the binary relation on $A = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR = \\{ (1,2), (1,3), (2,3), (3,3) \\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw a digraph corresponding to this relation. \n\nWe start by drawing three *nodes* corresponding to the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e85e2eeb4a3d0382687b0d551379492504fb45ecdd93a4cb6885799a0f799d.png)\n\n \n\nThe locations of the nodes can be arbitrary. However, we should ensure that the diagram is easy to read.\n\nWe now go through the elements of $R$ to decide where to place *arrows* and *loops*:\n\n- $(1,2)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 2$ to the digraph\n\n- $(1,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $1 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(2,3)\\in R,$ so we add the arrow $2 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\n- $(3,3)\\in R,$ so we add the loop $3 \\to 3$ to the digraph\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/34/34297fa91d8eab0e076e3b4e22359189b64271741cf2624e8535921fb6f8b302.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "177ebb26-050a-5d21-8e09-6eb5696d6240" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9075 :tutorial/title "Directed Graphs"} :step/id #uuid "f636e4f9-1c77-5713-ac0b-f2e7b50495da" :step/math-academy-id 156622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204695}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203685 :question/id #uuid "5c1451cc-7b5c-5f8f-9dfb-60c18319bce8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14614"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204231} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416071}] :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"} :step/id #uuid "a81964ff-60dc-5eb9-b608-34bfdd5a731d" :step/math-academy-id 156623}] :activity/title "Graphical Representations of Relations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186204120}]}
